Falsifies `system_tests::user::confined_fuzzer_tests::a_caller_chosen_list_cursor_is_refused`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64 (the hunk is portable kernel code, in the revocation log).

The defect this milestone's confined fuzzer found live (2026-10-06 UTC, seed 0x14): `AddressSpace::
LIST` passed its caller-supplied cursor straight into the revocation log's walk, and a cursor is
only ever `page_phys | index` for a page in this space's own chain. A confined process naming any
kernel-mapped page had it walked as a log page: a kernel data abort at best (what the fuzzer's
random cursor produced, `FAR_EL1 0xffff1e1d3b968008`), and at worst the page's contents returned as
mapping records, a kernel-memory read through a syscall register. The fix (the chain-membership
walk inside `revoke::list_mapping`'s one `SPACES` hold, refusing with `BadPointer` before anything
follows the word) is what this patch removes.

Expected to fail: the direct assertion ("a LIST cursor this space never minted was followed rather
than refused"); without the membership walk the kernel takes a data abort on the named page
instead, which is the original finding's own shape. Replayed red on aarch64 on 2026-10-06 (UTC) by
the lane for milestone 779 (fuzz the surface a confined process can reach).

**Re-cut 2026-10-07 (UTC) by the lane for milestone 139 (drive the unsafe count down)**, when the
log's walk moved behind `revoke::chain`, whose private cursor only the chain itself can set. The
defect cannot be written outside that module any more, which is the point of the move, so this
patch adds the one thing the fix took away, a walk that starts wherever its caller says
(`LogChain::pages_from`), and has `list_mapping` follow the cursor with it. The build warns that
`Listing::ForeignCursor` is never constructed; it does not fail. Replayed red on aarch64 the same day: the kernel took the data abort on the named page
(`exceptions.rs:772`), the original finding's shape.

